---
// VISUAILS — FigWalk: één bestelling van formulier tot download, als een vast
// kader bovenaan met negen stappen die eronder langsschuiven. Augustus 2026.
//
// WAAROM DIT BESTAAT. Lucas, met vijf schermafbeeldingen van een sticky
// betaalvisual erbij: *"Ik wil eigenlijk dat het een sticky page is en dat je
// dan 1 categorie selecteert … en dat je dan door het proces word geleidt."*
// En daarna, over de eerste versie hiervan: *"Ik zou graag willen dat de foto
// bovenaan het scherm sticky blijft en dat de tekst en de stylen van links
// naar rechts gaan net als lenis tijdens het scrollen. Veel white space en
// rust en duidelijk en gedetaileerd vertellen wat er precies gebeurt wanneer
// een klant besteld."*
//
// Vier dingen staan in die tweede zin en alle vier sturen de vorm.
//
//   1 · HET BEELD BOVENAAN, NIET ERNAAST. De eerste versie zette het kader in
//       een linkerkolom met de tekst rechts. Dat leest als een productpagina.
//       Nu plakt het kader onder de navigatie, gecentreerd, en loopt de tekst
//       eronder door — één kolom, één ding tegelijk in beeld.
//
//   2 · VAN LINKS NAAR RECHTS. Elke stap komt van links binnengeschoven, staat
//       stil terwijl je hem leest, en schuift naar rechts weg. Dat is geen
//       animatie met een eigen tijdlijn: de verschuiving hangt aan de
//       scrollpositie, dus hij beweegt precies zo snel als jij scrolt en erft
//       de vloeiendheid van Lenis (src/scripts/smooth-scroll.js), die de scroll
//       van deze site al lerpt. Vandaar "net als lenis".
//
//       Er is bewust GEEN horizontale baan gekozen die de scroll overneemt.
//       Dat is de vorm van veel awardsites en het is ook de vorm waar
//       trackpadgebruikers uit stappen; gevraagd, en het antwoord was de
//       zachte variant.
//
//   3 · VEEL WHITE SPACE EN RUST. Eén kolom, één maximale leesbreedte, en per
//       stap ruim een half scherm hoogte. De verschuiving is 72 pixels, niet
//       driehonderd: genoeg om te merken dat er iets beweegt, te weinig om
//       ernaar te moeten kijken.
//
//   4 · GEDETAILLEERD VERTELLEN WAT ER PRECIES GEBEURT. De vorige versie had
//       vijf stappen die pas bij de foto begonnen. Het formulier, de agenda,
//       de betaling, het portaal en de revisie ontbraken — en dat zijn juist
//       de dingen waar iemand die op het punt staat te bestellen zich zorgen
//       over maakt. Het zijn er negen nu, en de volgorde is de echte volgorde:
//       het venster wordt bevestigd VOOR de betaling. Zie WALK_STEPS in
//       src/data/demo.js.
//
// ── WAT DIT VERVING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FigFlow (de rail met prijsschuif en capaciteitspoort) en het demospel op
// /demo. Eén ding in plaats van drie, dus één fotoserie om te schieten. De
// prijsschuif en de poort zijn er op verzoek uitgehaald; de poort staat als
// belofte in de tekst boven de doorloop en de prijzen staan op /pricing.
//
// ── ALLES WORDT SERVERSIDE GERENDERD, NIETS WORDT GEMAAKT ────────────────────
// Elke laag van het kader, voor elke dienst, voor elke keuze, staat als markup
// in de pagina met `hidden` erop. Het script zet `hidden` om, zet een class, en
// schrijft een transform. Verder niets. De reden is de val waar dit project al
// drie keer in gelopen is: Astro's scoped CSS hangt aan `data-astro-cid-*`, dat
// alleen op template-elementen staat, dus een node die JavaScript maakt matcht
// geen enkele regel in dit bestand.
//
// ── ZONDER JAVASCRIPT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// De keuzeknoppen zijn `<input type="radio">` in `<label>`s, dus die werken.
// Wat niet werkt is het omschakelen van het kader. Daarom hangt zowel het
// sticky-gedrag als het verbergen van lagen achter `.js` op <html>: zonder
// script staan alle lagen gewoon onder elkaar en scrollt de pagina er langs.
// Lang, maar waar en volledig. Dezelfde afspraak als `.reveal.pending` in
// global.css.
//
// GEEN ARROW FUNCTIONS IN JSX-ATTRIBUTEN en GEEN GEÏNTERPOLEERDE CLASSNAMEN —
// zie de kop van FigPipeline.astro. Alles afgeleid wordt hieronder uitgerekend.
import {
  WALK_SERVICES, WALK_STEPS, WALK_COPY,
  DEMO_MODEL, DEMO_LOCKED_MODELS, DEMO_BACKGROUNDS, walkResult,
} from '../data/demo.js';
import { styles } from '../data/styles.js';
import { styles as stylesNl } from '../data/styles.nl.js';
import { videoStyles } from '../data/videoStyles.js';
import { videoStyles as videoStylesNl } from '../data/videoStyles.nl.js';
import { SHOTS } from '../data/shots.js';
import { localizedPath } from '../i18n/ui.js';

// LANG IS THE ONLY PROP, and this component localises its own links. The five
// retired figures on /how-it-works took no `lang` because they took no copy
// either — everything arrived as a prop, so parity was checkable in one file.
// This one has a copy table AND two host pages, so threading three hrefs
// through both of them would be the same paths typed twice with no checker on
// either. The copy lives in WALK_COPY for the same reason.
interface Props { lang: 'en' | 'nl' }
const { lang } = Astro.props;
const lp = (p: string) => localizedPath(lang, p);

const t = WALK_COPY[lang] || WALK_COPY.en;
const startHref = lp('/start');
const rosterHref = lp('/custom-models');
const sampleHref = lp('/test-sample');

// Style NAMES come from the style modules, never from demo.js — a style cannot
// be called "Dunes" here and something else on /lifestyle.
const lifeStyles = lang === 'nl' ? stylesNl : styles;
const vidStyles = lang === 'nl' ? videoStylesNl : videoStyles;
function nameOf(list, slug) {
  const hit = list.filter(function match(s) { return s.slug === slug; })[0];
  return hit ? hit.name : slug;
}
const bgHex = {};
for (const b of DEMO_BACKGROUNDS) bgHex[b.id] = b.hex;

// The whole tree, built in frontmatter. The markup below is then a nested map
// with no logic in it, which is the only way a template this size stays
// readable — and the only way the tag scanners can follow it.
const SERVICES = WALK_SERVICES.map(function build(svc) {
  const looks = svc.lookIds.map(function look(id) {
    let name = id;
    if (svc.look === 'background') name = t.backgroundNames[id] || id;
    else if (svc.look === 'style') name = nameOf(lifeStyles, id);
    else if (svc.look === 'videoStyle') name = nameOf(vidStyles, id);
    const set = walkResult(svc.id, id);
    return { id: id, name: name, hex: bgHex[id] || null, preview: set[0], set: set };
  });
  // THE FOUR SHOTS THE CUSTOMER SENDS, named from src/data/shots.js and not
  // from the delivery vocabulary. They are not the same words: `worn` is
  // "Worn" when you are being asked for a photo of it on someone, and "On a
  // model" when it is a frame we are handing back. The first pass used the
  // delivery names here and the upload step asked the reader for an image we
  // had not made yet. Only the first slot has a photograph; the other three
  // are hatched, and the caption under the figure says why.
  const shots = SHOTS.map(function shot(sh) {
    return { id: sh.id, name: sh.name[lang] || sh.name.en };
  });
  return {
    id: svc.id,
    out: svc.out,
    look: svc.look,
    source: svc.source,
    shots: shots,
    name: t.services[svc.id].name,
    line: t.services[svc.id].line,
    looks: looks,
    startLook: looks[0].id,
  };
});

// Catalog is what the server renders: four outputs is the fullest end state,
// and therefore the one worth showing to anyone whose JavaScript never arrives.
const START = SERVICES[0].id;
---

<section class="wk" data-walk aria-label={t.label}>

  {/* ── THE PICKER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Radios, not buttons: one of three, mutually exclusive, arrow-key
      navigable for free, and it survives with no script. */}
  <div class="wk-pick">
    {/* Een h2 en geen h3: op /demo is de <h1> de paginakop en was dit de eerstvolgende
        kop, dus sprong het niveau van 1 naar 3. De maat blijft die van .wk-pick-h en
        verandert dus niet. */}
    <h2 class="wk-pick-h">{t.pickH}</h2>
    <div class="wk-pick-row" role="radiogroup" aria-label={t.pickH}>
      {SERVICES.map((svc) => (
        <label class="wk-svc">
          <input type="radio" name="wk-service" value={svc.id} checked={svc.id === START} data-walk-service />
          <span class="wk-svc-box">
            <span class="wk-svc-name">{svc.name}</span>
            <span class="wk-svc-line">{svc.line}</span>
          </span>
        </label>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="wk-pick-hint">{t.pickHint}</p>
  </div>

  {SERVICES.map((svc) => (
    <div class="wk-body" data-walk-body={svc.id} hidden={svc.id !== START}>

      {/* ── THE STAGE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
          Sticky under the header, centred, with room around it. Every layer
          it can ever show is already here; the script only flips `hidden`.

          The wrapper carries the page ground and a fade at its foot, because
          the steps scroll UP BEHIND it — without that they would be legible
          through the gap beside the frame, which reads as a rendering fault
          rather than as depth. */}
      <div class="wk-stage">
        <div class="wk-frame">

          {/* order — the form. No photograph: there is nothing to photograph
              about a form, and a fake screenshot of one would be the invented
              proof every figure on this site has refused. */}
          <figure class="wk-layer is-panel" data-walk-layer="order">
            <ol class="wk-rail">
              {t.orderLines.map((l) => (
                <li><span class="wk-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>{l}</li>
              ))}
            </ol>
            <figcaption class="wk-cap">{t.capOrder}</figcaption>
          </figure>

          {/* upload — the four shots the customer sends */}
          <figure class="wk-layer is-shots" data-walk-layer="upload" hidden>
            <ul class="wk-src">
              {svc.shots.map((shot, i) => (
                <li>
                  {i === 0
                    ? <img src={svc.source.src} alt="" width="600" height="750" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                    : <span class="wk-src-drawn" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
                  <span class="wk-src-t">{shot.name}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
            <figcaption class="wk-cap">{t.capUpload}</figcaption>
          </figure>

          {/* look — one layer per option */}
          {svc.looks.map((look) => (
            <figure class="wk-layer" data-walk-layer="look" data-walk-look={look.id} hidden>
              <img src={look.preview.src} alt="" width="900" height="1200" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
              <figcaption class="wk-cap">{look.name}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))}

          {/* window — the gate, both sides. No scale, no bands, nothing to
              count: FigCapacity's rule, kept. The claim is "this can say no",
              not "here is how full next week is". */}
          <figure class="wk-layer is-panel" data-walk-layer="window" hidden>
            <div class="wk-gate">
              <p class="wk-pass"><span class="wk-tick" aria-hidden="true"></span>{t.windowPass}</p>
              <p class="wk-refuse"><span class="wk-ring" aria-hidden="true"></span>{t.windowRefuse}</p>
            </div>
            <figcaption class="wk-cap">{t.capWindow}</figcaption>
          </figure>

          {/* pay */}
          <figure class="wk-layer is-panel" data-walk-layer="pay" hidden>
            <p class="wk-pay">{t.payLine}</p>
            <figcaption class="wk-cap">{t.capPay}</figcaption>
          </figure>

          {/* model — two frames, because the claim is that a face is ADDED.
              A picture that replaces the product with a portrait says the
              opposite. */}
          <figure class="wk-layer is-pair" data-walk-layer="model" hidden>
            <img src={svc.looks[0].preview.src} alt="" width="900" height="1200" loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-walk-model-look />
            <img src={DEMO_MODEL.photo} alt="" width="800" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption class="wk-cap">{DEMO_MODEL.name}</figcaption>
          </figure>

          {/* make */}
          <figure class="wk-layer is-panel" data-walk-layer="make" hidden>
            <ul class="wk-checks">
              {t.checks.map((c) => (
                <li><span class="wk-tick" aria-hidden="true"></span>{c}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </figure>

          {/* portal — three states at once. FigApproval's parallelism, kept:
              a column of bullets is a picture of the opposite. The tile grid
              is aria-hidden and the legend carries the meaning as text. */}
          <figure class="wk-layer is-panel" data-walk-layer="portal" hidden>
            <ul class="wk-tiles" aria-hidden="true">
              <li class="wk-tile is-ok"></li>
              <li class="wk-tile is-rev"></li>
              <li class="wk-tile is-wait"></li>
              <li class="wk-tile is-ok"></li>
              <li class="wk-tile is-wait"></li>
              <li class="wk-tile is-ok"></li>
            </ul>
            <ul class="wk-legend">
              <li><span class="wk-key is-ok" aria-hidden="true"></span>{t.portalStates.ok}</li>
              <li><span class="wk-key is-rev" aria-hidden="true"></span>{t.portalStates.rev}</li>
              <li><span class="wk-key is-wait" aria-hidden="true"></span>{t.portalStates.wait}</li>
            </ul>
            <figcaption class="wk-cap">{t.capPortal}</figcaption>
          </figure>

          {/* result — one grid per look */}
          {svc.looks.map((look) => (
            <figure class="wk-layer is-out" data-walk-layer="result" data-walk-look={look.id} hidden>
              <ul class="wk-out" data-walk-count={String(svc.out)}>
                {look.set.map((frame) => (
                  <li class="wk-out-frame">
                    <img src={frame.src} alt="" width="900" height="1200" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                    {frame.shot && <span class="wk-out-t">{t.shotNames[frame.shot] || frame.shot}</span>}
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
              {svc.id === 'video' && look.set[0] && !look.set[0].clip
                ? <p class="wk-noclip">{t.noClip}</p>
                : null}
              <figcaption class="wk-cap">{t.outCount(svc.out)}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* ── THE STEPS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
          An <ol>, because the order carries meaning — the one case DESIGN.md's
          rejection of "01/02/03 scaffolding" exempts. The numbers are not
          printed; the sequence is the ordering. */}
      <ol class="wk-steps">
        {WALK_STEPS.map((id) => (
          <li class="wk-step" data-walk-step={id === 'look' ? svc.look : id}>
            <div class="wk-step-in">
              <p class="wk-step-n">{t.steps[id].n}</p>
              <h3 class="wk-step-h">{t.steps[id].h}</h3>
              <p class="wk-step-b">{t.steps[id].b}</p>

              {id === 'look' ? (
                <div class="wk-looks">
                  {svc.looks.map((look) => (
                    <label class="wk-look">
                      <input type="radio" name={`wk-look-${svc.id}`} value={look.id} checked={look.id === svc.startLook} data-walk-look-input />
                      <span class="wk-look-box">
                        {look.hex
                          ? <span class="wk-look-swatch" style={`background:${look.hex}`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
                          : <img class="wk-look-thumb" src={look.preview.src} alt="" width="200" height="200" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />}
                        <span class="wk-look-name">{look.name}</span>
                      </span>
                    </label>
                  ))}
                </div>
              ) : null}
              {id === 'look' && svc.look !== 'background'
                ? <p class="wk-note">{t.lookCustom}</p>
                : null}

              {id === 'model' ? (
                <div class="wk-models">
                  <ul class="wk-roster">
                    <li class="wk-face is-on">
                      <img src={DEMO_MODEL.photo} alt="" width="200" height="250" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                      <span>{DEMO_MODEL.name}</span>
                    </li>
                    {DEMO_LOCKED_MODELS.map((m) => (
                      <li class="wk-face">
                        <img src={m.photo} alt="" width="200" height="250" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                        <span>{m.name}</span>
                      </li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                  <p class="wk-note">{t.modelLocked} · <a href={rosterHref}>{t.modelRoster}</a></p>
                </div>
              ) : null}

              {id === 'result' ? (
                <div class="wk-cta">
                  <a href={sampleHref} class="btn btn-primary">{t.ctaPrimary}</a>
                  <a href={startHref} class="btn btn-ghost">{t.ctaGhost}</a>
                </div>
              ) : null}
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  ))}

  <p class="wk-honest">{t.placeholderNote}</p>
</section>

<script>
  // THE BEHAVIOUR LIVES WITH THE COMPONENT, not with the page. Two pages host
  // this — /demo and /how-it-works — and an import in each of them is an import
  // one of the two can be missing, which is exactly what happened on the first
  // build: /demo rendered a perfect static walkthrough that responded to
  // nothing. Astro hoists and de-duplicates component scripts, so it ships once
  // even when the component appears twice, and it does not reach the other 84
  // pages.
  import '../scripts/walk.js';
</script>

<style>
  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     FigWalk

     THE RULE THAT SHAPES EVERYTHING BELOW: the static form has to be readable.
     `position: sticky`, the hidden layers and the left-to-right slide are all
     applied only under `.js`, so with no script this is a plain column — every
     layer stacked, every step at normal reading rhythm. Get this backwards and
     a script failure leaves an empty box where the walkthrough was.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  .wk { margin: 0; }

  /* ── the picker ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .wk-pick { max-width: 34rem; margin: 0 auto clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem); text-align: center; }
  .wk-pick-h { font-size: .74rem; letter-spacing: .16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-4); margin: 0 0 1.1rem; font-weight: 500; }
  .wk-pick-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: .5rem; }
  .wk-pick-hint { margin: 1.2rem auto 0; max-width: 46ch; font-size: .82rem; color: var(--ink-4); line-height: 1.6; }

  /* Clipped, not display:none — that would take the radio out of the tab order
     and out of the accessibility tree, which is the usual way a "custom radio
     group" quietly stops being one. */
  .wk-svc input,
  .wk-look input {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .wk-svc { display: block; cursor: pointer; }
  .wk-svc-box {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: .15rem;
    padding: .85rem 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    transition: border-color var(--dur-1) var(--ease), background var(--dur-1) var(--ease);
  }
  .wk-svc-name { font-size: .98rem; }
  .wk-svc-line { font-size: .76rem; color: var(--ink-4); }
  .wk-svc input:checked + .wk-svc-box { border-color: var(--accent); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 7%, transparent); }
  .wk-svc input:focus-visible + .wk-svc-box { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; }

  .wk-body[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* ── the stage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* THE WRAPPER CARRIES THE GROUND, not just the frame. The steps scroll up
     behind this, and without an opaque backing they stay legible in the gap
     either side of the frame — which reads as a rendering fault, not as depth.
     `--bg-seam` and not `--bg-0`: the visible ground on this site is lifted to
     ≈#0F1011 by the fixed grain-and-lamp layer in global.css, so painting
     #08090B here would show as a darker rectangle. The mask fades the last
     couple of rem so the edge is not a hard line across the page. */
  :global(.js) .wk-stage {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    padding: clamp(4.4rem, 7vh, 5.4rem) 0 clamp(1.4rem, 3vh, 2.2rem);
  }
  /* FULL-BLEED, because this element sits inside a .container and the ground it
     is hiding does not. Painting the background on .wk-stage itself left a
     visible vertical seam at the container edge — the flat fill against the
     lamp-lit ground either side of it. The pseudo-element escapes the container
     the way the rest of this site does it: 100vw, pulled back half a viewport.
     100vw and not 100% because a scrollbar makes those differ, and a hair of
     horizontal overflow is cheaper than a hair of ground showing through. */
  :global(.js) .wk-stage::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0 auto 0 50%;
    width: 100vw;
    margin-left: -50vw;
    z-index: -1;
    background: var(--bg-seam);
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 calc(100% - 2.6rem), transparent 100%);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 calc(100% - 2.6rem), transparent 100%);
  }

  /* ONE COLUMN WIDTH FOR THE WHOLE THING. The frame was 30rem and the steps
     46rem, both centred — so they shared a centre line and nothing else, and
     every heading started 128px to the left of the picture above it. Two
     centred blocks of different widths do not read as one column; they read as
     two things laid out separately. 34rem is wide enough for a comfortable
     measure (about sixty characters at this size) and narrow enough that the
     result grid stays legible, so the frame and the text now share both edges. */
  .wk-frame {
    position: relative;
    width: min(100%, 34rem);
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    /* A fixed shape, so the sticky box does not resize under the reader as the
       layers swap. A stage that grows and shrinks while you scroll past it is
       the single thing that makes this pattern feel broken. */
    min-height: min(26rem, 46svh);
  }

  /* Under .js every layer sits in the same grid cell and one is shown. Without
     .js they lay out in flow, one under the other, all visible. */
  :global(.js) .wk-layer { grid-area: 1 / 1; }
  .wk-layer { margin: 0; width: 100%; }

  .wk-layer > img, .wk-layer > picture > img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-height: min(22rem, 40svh);
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
  }

  .wk-cap { margin-top: .8rem; font-size: .78rem; color: var(--ink-4); text-align: center; }

  /* Panels — the steps with nothing to photograph. Deliberately quiet: a border
     and text, no invented screenshot. */
  .wk-layer.is-panel {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    min-height: min(22rem, 40svh);
    padding: clamp(1.2rem, 3vw, 1.8rem);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .wk-rail { list-style: none; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: .85rem; }
  .wk-rail li,
  .wk-checks li { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .7rem; font-size: .92rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .wk-checks { list-style: none; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: .85rem; }
  .wk-dot { flex: 0 0 auto; width: .45rem; height: .45rem; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--line-ui); }
  .wk-tick { flex: 0 0 auto; width: .8rem; height: .8rem; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--accent); }
  .wk-ring { flex: 0 0 auto; width: .8rem; height: .8rem; border-radius: 50%; border: 1px solid var(--line-strong); }

  .wk-gate { display: grid; gap: 1rem; text-align: left; }
  .wk-pass,
  .wk-refuse { display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: .65rem; margin: 0; font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .wk-pass span,
  .wk-refuse span { margin-top: .3rem; }
  .wk-refuse { color: var(--ink-4); }
  .wk-pay { margin: 0; text-align: center; font-size: .95rem; color: var(--ink-2); }

  /* the model pair */
  .wk-layer.is-pair { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: .5rem; }
  .wk-layer.is-pair .wk-cap { grid-column: 1 / -1; }

